[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we can use Ohm's law, which states that the voltage \( V \) across a resistor is equal to the current \( I \) through the resistor multiplied by the resistance \( R \) of the resistor. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V = IR \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m We are given that when \( R = 3 \) ohms, the current \( I = 40 \) amperes. We can use this information to find the voltage \( V \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V = I \times R = 40 \, \text{A} \times 3 \, \text{ohms} = 120 \, \text{volts} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Now, we need to find the current \( I \) when the resistance \( R \) is 20 ohms. Using Ohm's law again, we have:
