[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m A particle moves so that it is at $\left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)$ at time $t.$  Find the speed of the particle, measured in unit of distance per unit of time. Let's think step by step and output the final answer within \boxed{}.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m [response] To find the speed of the particle, we need to determine the magnitude of its velocity vector. The velocity vector is the derivative of the position vector with respect to time.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m The position of the particle at time \( t \) is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m First, we find the velocity components by differentiating each component of the position vector with respect to \( t \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4} \right) = 3 \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} = \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right) = 3 \cdot \left( -\frac{1}{4} \right) \sin \frac{t}{4} = -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m So, the velocity vector is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4}, -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Next, we find the speed of the particle, which is the magnitude of the velocity vector:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \text{Speed} = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)^2 + \left( -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)^2}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \right)^2 \left( \cos^2 \frac{t}{4} + \sin^2 \frac{t}{4} \right)}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \right)^2 \cdot 1}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16}}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m = \frac{3}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Therefore, the speed of the particle is \(\boxed{\frac{3}{4}}\) units of distance per unit of time.
