[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    - In an isosceles triangle, the altitudes from the vertex angle to the base are equal and intersect at the orthocenter.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    - The altitudes from the base angles to the opposite sides are also equal and intersect at the midpoint of the base.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 2. **Understand the intersection of the altitudes:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    - The altitudes from the vertex angle and the base angles intersect on the incircle of the triangle. This means that the orthocenter (the intersection of the altitudes) and the incenter (the intersection of the angle bisectors) coincide.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 3. **Use the properties of the orthocenter and incenter:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    - In an isosceles triangle, the orthocenter, circumcenter, and incenter are all the same point. This is a special property of isosceles triangles.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    - For an isosceles triangle, the vertex angle is twice the base angle. Let the base angle be \( \theta \). Then the vertex angle is \( 2\theta \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 4. **Determine the cosine of the vertex angle:**
