[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    The function \( f(x) = \sqrt{x^2} \) can be simplified because the square root of a square is the absolute value of the original number. Therefore, \( \sqrt{x^2} = |x| \). So, the function becomes \( f(x) = |x| \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 2. **Determine the range of the absolute value function:**
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m    The absolute value function \( |x| \) outputs the non-negative value of \( x \). This means that for any real number \( x \), \( |x| \) will always be greater than or equal to 0. Specifically:
