[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we start with the physics formula for force, which is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \text{Force} = \text{mass} \times \text{acceleration} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Let's denote the force as \( F \), the mass of the softball as \( m_s = 200 \) g, and the mass of the baseball as \( m_b = 150 \) g. We need to find the ratio of the acceleration of the softball to the acceleration of the baseball, which we will denote as \( \frac{a_s}{a_b} \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m From the formula, we can express the acceleration of each ball as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ F = m_s \times a_s \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ F = m_b \times a_b \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m We can solve for the accelerations by dividing the first equation by the second:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \frac{F}{m_b} = \frac{m_s \times a_s}{m_b \times a_b} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \frac{F}{m_b} = \frac{m_s}{m_b} \times \frac{a_s}{a_b} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Rearranging to solve for the ratio \( \frac{a_s}{a_b} \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \frac{a_s}{a_b} = \frac{F}{m_b} \times \frac{m_b}{m_s} = \frac{F}{m_s} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Now, we substitute the given masses:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \frac{a_s}{a_b} = \frac{F}{200 \text{ g}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m Since the force \( F \) is the same for both balls, the ratio of the accelerations is simply the ratio of the masses:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \frac{a_s}{a_b} = \frac{150 \text{ g}}{200 \text{ g}} = \frac{150}{200} = \frac{3}{4} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m So, the ratio of the acceleration of Jen's ball to Jack's is \( \frac{3}{4} \).
