[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m [response] To determine how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill a spherical tank, we first need to calculate the volumes of both the cylindrical beaker and the spherical tank.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m The volume \( V \) of a cylinder is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi r^2 h \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m where \( r \) is the radius and \( h \) is the height. For the cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ r = 3 \text{ cm}, \quad h = 8 \text{ cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi (3)^2 (8) = 72\pi \text{ cm}^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m The volume \( V \) of a sphere is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi R^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m where \( R \) is the radius. For the spherical tank:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ R = 6 \text{ cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi (6)^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi (216) = 288\pi \text{ cm}^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m To find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=55750)[0m \[ \text{Number of beakers} = \frac{V_{\text{sphere}}}{V_{\text{cylinder}}} = \frac{288\pi}{72\pi} = \frac{288}{72} = 4 \]
